Hi.  You've all showed some interest in working on the website thingy.
Here are some notes to start out.

Goals:

My own goals for this are to help people, to have some fun, and to get
myself set up as an obscenely expensive web consultant. :) At a more
concrete level, we want to provide whatever useful services there are
that can exploit a unified database of transit schedules, routes, and
users.  This includes rideshare matchmaking and public-transit trip
planning.  It could also include other stuff -- for example, what if
you could ask for a list of all people who've registered as
boardgamers within a 15-minute drive of your house?  (Are there web
services like that yet?  The geographical aspect is what I haven't
seen.)

The database will initially be limited to the Bay Area, but the
software shouldn't be so restricted.

The other concrete goal is to release our code as free software, and
our transit data format as a candidate de facto standard for other
people to use -- vastly extending the reach of the software without
tons more work on our part.

All this should be taken as how I'm seeing this, of course, not a set
of marching orders -- more ideas would be great.

It turns out www.transitinfo.org is already working on the
public-transit trip planning, but they're taking their time going live
with it.  I think the thing to do is mail them and try to find out
their status and whether we can work with them, or maybe even find out
there's nothing left for us to do.


Resources:

http://www.transitinfo.org is one -- they've brought together a lot of
data on public transit routes and schedules in a common format.

http://photo.net/wtr/thebook
A book on building web services that I recommend to all of you.  I'm
going to be reviewing relevant parts, too, to refresh my memory.

http://sunsite.Berkeley.EDU/GovData/info/tiger.html
Has 1997 TIGER/Line files for the whole country from the U.S. Census.
This is basically a street map with latitude/longitude and postal
address data.

http://tiger.census.gov/
This server can generate street maps for us until we have our own code
for that (maybe adapted from theirs if we're lucky).  There seems to
be no way to overlay a route over the map using their API, so it's not
a complete solution.

There's a GPLed program to convert Tiger files to some DOS map format
I've never heard of.  (Don't have URL handy.)  I'll be using it as my
starting point for the geocoding (finding latitude/longitude from
street address) and map generation.  It's in C and too monolithic --
it'll have to get taken apart.  Also it's for the 1994 version of
